    </svg>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     TIME DIFFERENCE & METHOD CARDS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section content-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
  <h2 id="method-heading">How to Convert MEST to EST</h2>
  <p class="method-intro">
    Converting European summer time (MEST, UTC+2) to standard US Eastern time (EST, UTC-5) requires subtracting 7 hours.
  </p>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 1</p>
      <h3>Direct 7-Hour Subtraction</h3>
      <p>
        Subtract 7 hours from your MEST clock. Example: A call scheduled for 4:00 PM MEST in Paris becomes 9:00 AM EST in New York.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 2</p>
      <h3>The Late Night Date Rollback Rule</h3>
      <p>
        Early morning hours in MEST (between 12:00 AM Midnight and 6:59 AM) subtract 7 hours to land on the late afternoon or evening of the previous day in EST (e.g., 3:00 AM MEST Tuesday = 8:00 PM EST Monday).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 3</p>
      <h3>Summer Overlap (EDT) Adjustment</h3>
      <p>
        When North America observes Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4) during summer, subtract only 6 hours from MEST instead of 7 hours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

